# A file and a directory of the same name, which used to be a repository the
# toolchain refused to read.
#
# `lib/money/cents.buri` is a module of //lib/money and `lib/money/cents/` is a
# package of its own. While a module path was a name with the file left off,
# both were spelled "//lib/money/cents" and one of the two had to be renamed:
# `package-shadows-a-module`, rule 3 of LIBRARIES.md.
#
# Now an import names a file. "//lib/money/cents.buri" is the module and
# "//lib/money/cents/lib.buri" is the package's surface, they are two strings,
# and there is nothing left to refuse. This case is kept — under a new name —
# so that the layout stays *tested* rather than merely untested: it is the
# sharpest thing the change bought, and a resolver that quietly went back to
# resolving both to one file would fail here.
#
# //lib/money/rounding is the same shape without the file beside it, so the
# case says something about both halves of the old rule.
#
# Schema: the repository-case manifest, cli/tests/harness/case.rs. Textproto,
# but not a Buri file: only repo/ below is a Buri repository.

doc: "a module and a package of the same name are two module paths, because each names its own file"

run {
  args: ["build", "//..."]
  exit: 0
}

# The module, not the package one directory down. If the two ever collapsed
# back into one spelling, whichever won would take `fromCents` away from the
# other and this would stop building.
run {
  args: ["lint", "//..."]
  exit: 0
}
